          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582783,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Early Years of Dorothy Hodgkin</strong><br>\nDorothy Hodgkin, born in 1910 in Cairo, Egypt, to British parents, was fascinated by chemistry from a young age. She studied at the University of Oxford and then at Cambridge University, where she developed her lifelong interest in the structure of biological molecules.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Introduction to X-ray Crystallography</strong><br>\nHodgkin was a pioneer in the field of X-ray crystallography, a technique used to determine the three-dimensional structures of crystals. She used this method to understand the structures of important biochemical substances, laying the groundwork for her future discoveries.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Breakthrough with Insulin</strong><br>\nHodgkin's most famous work involved deciphering the structure of insulin, a hormone crucial for regulating sugar in the blood. This breakthrough took many years and had significant implications for diabetes treatment and understanding.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "53bc5361-5ce5-43a9-a00f-57bbceee9cd0"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          }
        ],
