          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Academic Legacy and Mentorship</strong><br>\nAs a professor at the University of Oxford, Hodgkin mentored many students who would go on to make their own significant scientific contributions. She was dedicated to supporting the next generation of scientists, particularly women in science.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Recognition and Honors</strong><br>\nHodgkin received numerous honors throughout her career, including being the first woman to receive the Order of Merit in 1965 and the Copley Medal of the Royal Society. Her work garnered her international acclaim and numerous honorary degrees.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Dorothy Hodgkin's Enduring Impact</strong><br>\nDorothy Hodgkin passed away in 1994, but her legacy endures. Her contributions to the field of chemistry, especially in the understanding of the structure of key biological molecules, continue to influence scientific research and medical advancements today.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
